**Appendix C: Policy Alignment Summary**

*Government policy requirements supporting digital pedagogy capability*

January 2026

**Executive Summary**

Government policy direction in 2025-2026 has significantly strengthened requirements around digital pedagogy, accessibility, and inclusive teaching. The timing of the proposed restructure coincides with these heightened expectations. This summary provides verifiable sources for independent review.

**Key Policy Timeline**

| **Date** | **Policy Development** |
| --- | --- |
| **January 2025** | DfE announces plans to modernise education sector with technology, including mandatory AT training for new teachers |
| **February 2025** | Digital Inclusion Action Plan published - local delivery with colleges as partners |
| **July 2025** | DfE Accessibility & Inclusive Design Manual updated (21 July 2025) |
| **September 2025** | Mandatory assistive technology training for new teachers commences |
| **October 2025** | Post-16 Education and Skills White Paper published |
| **November 2025** | New Ofsted Education Inspection Framework operational (10 November 2025) |
| **Now** | TLA Excellence Framework launching at Weston College this academic year |

**1. Ofsted Education Inspection Framework (November 2025)**

The new EIF replaces single-word grades with report cards across evaluation areas including Inclusion and Curriculum & teaching. Providers must evidence accessible teaching, strong digital pedagogy, and equitable participation.

**Relevance to Digital Coach role: **The role directly supports evidence generation for Inclusion and Curriculum & teaching evaluation areas through Learning Walks, QIP support, and staff coaching.

**Source: **https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/education-inspection-framework/education-inspection-framework-for-use-from-november-2025

**2. DfE Accessibility ****&**** Inclusive Design Manual (July 2025)**

Updated 21 July 2025, this manual consolidates standards, tooling, audits, and role-based guidance to ensure education services meet WCAG and inclusive design expectations. It mandates accessible digital practice, not just accessible platforms.

**Relevance to Digital Coach role: **While technical compliance is the Accessibility Manager's domain, translating accessibility principles into classroom practice is pedagogical coaching - the Digital Coach function.

**Source: **https://accessibility.education.gov.uk/

**Blog update: **https://accessibility.blog.gov.uk/2025/09/16/improving-the-dfe-accessibility-and-inclusive-design-manual/

**3. Mandatory Assistive Technology Training (September 2025)**

All new teachers must undergo mandatory training in how to effectively use assistive technology to support children with SEND. This requirement commenced in September 2025 and requires provider-level capability to deliver and support this training.

**Relevance to Digital Coach role: **The role supports AT training through coaching and CPD delivery. The PGCE digital pedagogy component is directly aligned to this requirement.

**Source: **https://www.tes.com/magazine/news/general/dfe-edtech-plans-for-schools-mandatory-assistive-tech-training-all-new-teachers-2025

**4. EEF Evidence on Digital Technology and Attainment**

The Education Endowment Foundation found that when used effectively, digital technology can accelerate learning by 2 to 3 months. The DfE cites this evidence in its strategy. The key qualifier is "when used effectively" - this requires pedagogical capability, not just technical provision.

**Relevance to Digital Coach role: **The coaching role exists precisely to ensure digital tools are used effectively. Technical provision alone does not deliver the attainment gains.

**Source: **https://educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk/education-evidence/guidance-reports/digital

**5. Post-16 Education and Skills White Paper (October 2025)**

The White Paper prioritises FE as a prestigious, specialist sector with emphasis on provider specialism, professional development, and employer partnerships. Digital capability is central to implementing curriculum reforms and innovation.

**Relevance to Digital Coach role: **Staff development in digital pedagogy directly supports the White Paper's professional development priorities.

**Source: **https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/post-16-education-and-skills-white-paper/post-16-education-and-skills-white-paper

**Conclusion**

The consultation letter references recent updates to the Education Inspection Framework and the Further Education White Paper. However, it does not appear to account for the strengthened position on digital pedagogy, accessibility, and inclusive practice in these and related policy documents.

Three major requirements are already operational (EIF, Mandatory AT training, Accessibility Manual), and the college initiative (TLA Excellence Framework) is underway. Removing pedagogical coaching capability at this moment creates a gap precisely when policy requires this function to be strengthened.

*All sources cited above are publicly accessible government publications that can be independently verified.*